  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nCenter of expansive mid level ridge will slowly shift northeast\ncloser to the forecast area over the next few days. This will keep \nhot and humid conditions across the region. There has been little \nchange in thinking with the forecast. Peak heat indices will mostly \nbe in the 100 to 105 range. There will no doubt be isolated spots \nthat exceed 105, but not enough to warrant deviating from the \nprolonged heat advisory for now. However, there remains the \npossibility that as newer data comes in, an upgrade to an extreme \nheat warning for part or all of the area may be needed. There also\nremains the potential for heat advisory conditions to continue into\nFriday, but there continues to be uncertainty whether convection by\nthat time could keep conditions from reaching that threshold.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \nMid level ridge starts to get suppressed on Friday. This may be \nsufficient to allow for storms to develop in the afternoon and \nevening. Over the weekend, the area will firmly be on the southern \nedge of the westerlies. During the warm season, this is a prime \nlocation for storm complexes as disturbances ripple through that flow\ntapping into a high theta e airmass. So the chance of storms will \nincrease for the holiday weekend. But it will keep heat indices \nslightly lower. Storms will bring the potential for some severe \nweather, wind being the greater threat, although various AI \nconvective forecasts as well as the CIPS are targeting different days\nfor when the potential will be greatest.",
